// ★ `@io func` ≡ `proc` — the first step to ONE INTRODUCER.
//
// The endgame: `func` is pure and total by DEFAULT, and every deviation is acknowledged by its
// own attribute — `@io` to perform IO, `@diverges` to be allowed not to return. `proc` then has
// nothing left to say and can go.
//
// ★ AN ANNOTATION NEVER TELLS THE COMPILER SOMETHING IT COULD INFER. Effects are always
// inferred, here and before. The attribute's job is CONSENT: this function deviates from a
// guarantee the language gives by default, and the compiler checks the deviation was intended.
// That is why it is `@io` and not `effects io` — the row is a COMPLETE upper bound (declare one
// bit, owe them all), which is right for a bound and wrong for per-property consent.
//
// Both spellings work while the corpus migrates. `std/` is already in this form: its eight
// procs with bodies are `@io func` as of 2026-09-25.
extern proc libc_printf(fmt *u8, ...) i32
@io
func shout(x i32) i32 {
    return libc_printf("%d\n", x)
}
proc main() i32 { return shout(7) }
